Needlestick / Sharps Injury Assessment
General Practice Other
Percutaneous exposure to discarded sharp, Epidermal barrier break, Unknown source status, Baseline blood-borne virus serology

VISIT CONTEXT
Case ID: GPO-007
Reason for Visit: Acute percutaneous exposure to an unknown biological hazard with profound resulting psychological distress.
Location: Private residence
Present at Visit: Patient only
Urgency Justification: Telephone triage reported a recent, deep percutaneous injury from a discarded hypodermic needle found in a public park, triggering an acute panic response. Urgent face-to-face assessment was strictly required to irrigate the wound, assess the exact depth of the puncture, evaluate the immediate requirement for post-exposure prophylaxis (PEP) within the critical efficacy window, and arrest the severe acute anxiety. Delaying this assessment until the next in-hours period risked exceeding the ideal window for PEP initiation and leaving the patient in a state of unmanageable distress overnight.

SUBJECTIVE
Chief Complaint: "I stepped on a needle in the park grass two hours ago, it bled, and I am absolutely terrified I have caught HIV."
History of Present Illness: The patient reports a recent percutaneous exposure to an inappropriately discarded sharp object. Two hours prior to the consultation, the patient stepped on a syringe while walking barefoot. This caused a definitive break in the epidermal barrier resulting in a localized puncture wound and minor bleeding. The patient reports immediate washing of the wound with soap and water upon returning home. The infectious status of the source object is completely unknown, leading to heightened patient anxiety regarding blood-borne virus transmission. The patient is hyperventilating and pacing the room.
Functional Status: Independent, but currently exhibiting acute pacing and psychomotor agitation due to fear.
Social/Safety Concerns: Patient lives alone; high risk of acute situational insomnia and spiraling health anxiety.

OBJECTIVE
Vitals: BP: 145/90 | HR: 115 | Temp: 36.8 | RR: 22 | SpO2: 99% | Wt: 72 Kg
General: Highly anxious, diaphoretic, fixated on the injured foot.
Physical Exam: Integumentary: Examination of the right plantar aspect reveals a 1mm puncture wound. Assessment of the depth and extent of the puncture injury indicates a subcutaneous depth of approximately 3mm with minimal surrounding erythema and no active bleeding. The presentation satisfies the criteria of percutaneous exposure to a discarded sharp with an epidermal barrier break and an unknown source status. Baseline blood-borne virus serology is required.
Environmental Observations: Clean, safe home environment; the discarded needle was safely secured in a hard plastic container by the patient and is present for inspection.
Medication Reconciliation: Nil regular medications.

ASSESSMENT
Primary Diagnosis: Community Needlestick / Sharps Injury Assessment - Status: Stable (Physiologically)
Differentials:

Retained Foreign Body - Ruled out/unlikely due to: Clear visualization of the intact needle tip brought by the patient, and no palpable mass or sharp pain upon deep palpation of the wound.

Acute Cellulitis - Ruled out/unlikely due to: Extremely brief timeframe since injury, absence of spreading erythema, and normal body temperature.

Tetanus Infection - Ruled out/unlikely due to: Immediate acute presentation lacking trismus or muscle spasms, though prophylactic risk exists.
Risk Level: Environmental/Social Risk: Low

CLINICAL REASONING SUMMARY
The patient sustained a definitive percutaneous exposure to a high-risk community biological hazard, presenting with acute tachycardia and hyperventilation secondary to severe health anxiety. Telephone triage correctly identified the time-sensitive nature of potential blood-borne pathogen exposure. Urgent face-to-face assessment was clinically mandated to physically examine the wound for retained foreign bodies, review tetanus and hepatitis B immunization status, and provide evidence-based counselling regarding the indications for PEP. Deferring this assessment until the next in-hours period would have caused unacceptable psychological harm and potentially missed the optimal pharmacological window for prophylactic intervention.

CLINICIAN CARE PLAN
Clinical Interventions: Thorough wound irrigation with chlorhexidine and sterile dressing application. Administered Tetanus toxoid booster IM (deltoid) as the previous booster was greater than 10 years ago. Conducted extensive discussion of the exceedingly low but non-zero risk of HIV, Hepatitis B, and Hepatitis C transmission from environmental sharps. Determined HIV PEP was not clinically indicated based on national guidelines for dried community needles.
Positive Behavioral Change: To manage the acute anxiety surrounding the risk of infection, commit to writing down the specific statistical risks discussed tonight on a piece of paper and placing it on your bedside table. Whenever a catastrophic thought regarding HIV occurs tonight, physically pick up the paper and read the facts aloud. This interrupts the cycle of catastrophic thinking with objective medical reality, actively reinforcing cognitive grounding.
Home-Based Services (Optional): None indicated.
DME/Safety (Optional): None.
Follow-up: Routine GP review within 48 hours to order baseline serum serology for HIV, Hepatitis B surface antigen/antibody, and Hepatitis C antibody.

SAFETY-NETTING ESCALATION PLAN
Call 000 or attend the Emergency Department immediately if you develop sudden, spreading red streaks up your leg, extreme throbbing pain in the foot, or a high fever with uncontrollable shivering.

PATIENT INFORMATION
Disclaimer: Diagnosis is presumptive pending investigations; conditions are dynamic.
Clinical Course: The puncture wound will heal over 3 to 5 days. Anxiety is expected to decrease following the clinical reassurance provided regarding the viability of viruses outside the human body.
SAFETY-NETTING ADVICE
Seek immediate medical attention if the puncture site begins discharging yellow pus or becomes exquisitely painful to bear weight on tomorrow.
PATIENT EDUCATION
Treatment Explanation: The tetanus shot prevents bacterial infection from the soil. We are not starting HIV medication because the virus dies rapidly when exposed to air, making the risk from a dried park needle virtually zero.
Actionable Steps: Keep the dressing clean and dry for 24 hours. Book a blood test with your regular GP within the next two days to establish your baseline health status.

